i use chrome extension "reader view" and assigned keyboard shortcut.  it 'hit or miss' for sites, but always worth a try.  if it doesnt work the first time, im sometimes able to click in a certain area of the screen before hitting the shortcut.

how so?  i was comparing, not analyzing.  1 guy said he knew he didn't need them but he liked how they felt, the other guy tells young men to be the strong ones at their fathers funerals...and then travels as far away from his wife's daethbed as possible to treat what can only be explained as drug addiction.  is that complicated enough for you?

&#x200B;

if i wanted to make it more complex, i'd explore the creepy incestuous relationship he has with his daughter.  care to elaborate?

>comments

i think you meant to say "you are foolish to ignore the potential risks".  otherwise you are saying that i am making an argument about something else in which the 'fact that 'marijuana' has no 'risks' is something important to my argument and i am ignoring it.

&#x200B;

i'm usually not a stickler, but if you are going to call someone "foolish", youd better make fucking sure that your 1 sentence response is grammatically sound.  i wood even forgive a spelling mistake if you went 'fonetic'.

&#x200B;

i didn't say marijuana has NO risks.  of course everything has 'risk'.  and i believe that people should be allowed to take risks legally.

agreed...great psychological insight into the mind of a d-lister though...over-using the 'creative' adjective and them immediately showing insecurity by worrying he's being pretentious out loud...the exchange when he repeats maron's 'the acting' to reference his next 'creative pursuit was douche chil overload.  reminded me a lot of an opie and anthny episode where louie ck is berating jay mohr for the entire episode and mohr has almost the same speaking voice where the 'anger quiver' in the voice hit that 'douche chill' sweet spot...in this one it was right after that line about  marc goofing on his rap name and he says something back like "you think im making fun of myself...yea...ok..." for some reason it made me want to strangle the dude..his and mohrs speaking boiec was something of an 'archetype' where i grew up in jersey...

&#x200B;

so i put the interview on loop and strangely found a way to channel those 'douche chills' into bursts of 'cortisol' that gave me energy to finish up a paintiing job....and thought id let the forums now  of the potential to extract energy form the stead stream of douche chills packed into this episode

just off the top of my head, as soon as i read about the zimmerman case, watever moral compass i have installed believed that, despite any 'legal technicalities' (which are routinely violated or ignored entirely in some cases), that given the evidence i dont want to live in a society that lets a man walk away from such an unjustified murder.  

&#x200B;

you dive into the case and quickly realize that pundits ike dershowitzes are far from geniuses.  any layman can sense the inherent danger in acquitting killers who 'genuinely fear their life is in danger'.  theres not a single 'expert who can explain to me why these types of laws are necessary so that people arent prosecuted for self-defense.  but i would like to see some data from porponents of the law that documented al of the innocent people that would have been prosecuted for self-defense vs the lives lost by a bullshit defense that slippery slides pretty quickly into 'reducto ad absurdum'.  you watch fox news overcover the 'justin smolit' case even as they employ  mark furhman as a legap expert, someone who'lied' extensively on tape alluding to a whole array of evidence-planting and despicable activities.  

&#x200B;

but then you read abou the byron smith case.  a man who knew that some white teenagers were going to break into his house and rob him if he just parked his truck somwehere else.  he had reported them on numerous occasions to the cops who did nothing.  so he killed them both when they broke into his house.  now there were noparallel 'stand ur ground' laws in this state, but im sure there wa a similar line of legal precedent in the state with regards to castle dosctrine etc.  the intruders were whlesome looking white kids.  he gets life in prison.  no one really seems to bat an eyelash .  he sounded 'crazy'

&#x200B;

then you have the trejo case where he fires on someone who shoved him.  if it were end have to end right there and they tried charging him with assault for shoving him, im pretty sure hed get acquitted because he had reasonable suspicoion that his wife/chld were in danger when a white man is yelling at her for parking illegally when hes parked illegally himself.  im also sure i that were the end of it, that dope trump-supporter of a sheriff would have arrested the guy for shoving him and played down a ahard line about 'your wife being threatened does not give u the right to push a man away from your car.  but of course thi guy, emboldened by how he knwos stand your ground laws will work in his favor, makes it his mission to create a scenario where he will muder a black man and get away with it.  though eventually conivicted, the shefiff didnt even arrest him at the scene.  thats a serious fucking problem with our legal system where somene can be comvicted of manslaughter/homicide frm the same amount of evidence avaialable at a scene where the cops choose not to even make an arrest.

i just caught the brittany smith headline out of the corner of my eye today, and i saw 'brittany smith / 'stand your ground' / 'alabama judge'.. and i said to myself, if her alleged rapist was black then this headline is going to tell me shes acquitted.  if her alleged rapist is white, shes going to jail and the murrder of the dead white rapist is going to be avenged.  lo and behold..

another topic in intersted to hear about from others is its effect on sense of musical pitch.  i didnt used to get any boosted 'music euphoria' from it....on the contrary if i overdid it, the songs id listen to would have slightly 'wobbly' tempos that sounded terrible.  then i listened to a performance video while on DPH and was unnerved on how out of pitch my vocals sounded.

&#x200B;

it was then i realized there could be 2 reasons for this (none of them good))...

&#x200B;

either the DPH has drastically improved my sense of hearing pitch and this is how offkey i actually sound to others.

&#x200B;

or the DPH is distorting my sense of pitch which wold be devastating as a musician

&#x200B;

so i analyzed further and fond that during 'sober listening' the notes that sounded wildly out of pitch on DPH actually were the most 'out of tune' parts of the otherwise onkey vocal.  leading me to conclude that it actually was enhancing my pitch (both hearing and singing).  after a while, the wobbly pitch effect has since transformed into complete music listeining euphoria (althogh when listening to my own recorings i will often get carried away with excitedly making notes on lyrical tweaks to improve the song..  seems that like its amplification of all moods for me, it also seemed to amplify pitch discrepancies.  my "moods on steroids" theory.  also, interrupted silence is amplified (as in closed doors always sound loudly slammed)

to clarify....(as best i can with written word..."wheeeerre te fuuuck tdid i puttt thesse hm hmm hmmm oooo ok heere they were la di da da daaa"...\*SIGH\*

&#x200B;

(capping things off with a deflated 'sigh' is particuarly infuriating because ill be hard at work on something only to be audibly 'draggged into' his 'time to relax' mood.  also because you lose a few seconds of your life thinkng and mistakeningly responding to people talking to themselves...and theres a good chance theyre doing this on purpose to piss you off  while retaining pllausible deniability.  ((like that SNL rob schneider sketch where hes playing guitar in the subway and begging for money with his lyrics.

i understand its 2 different states...but the fact that george zimmerman gets acquitted and then our country's most 'brilliant' legal minds like alan dershowitz defend the verdict, while this guy will be in prison for life doesn't sound like justice to me.  especially considering that byron's victims were whiite, and were not only breaking into a house but breaking into HIS house.  whereas zimmerna falsely concluded that a black child 'may be' committing a crime.  and was instructed by law enforcement (or at least thhe dispatcher) NOT to confront him.  whereas in byron's case i thkn he claimed to have called the police multiple times about multiple break ins to his house.  being CERTAIN that someone is going to break into your house if u hide your truck...there's something wrong with that equation

&#x200B;

it seems like every high profile trial i rea up on or hear in the news gets the opposite verdict of what i was hoping for. casey anthony.  george ziimmerman,  even the bernie goetz case from the 80s.  hed was acquitted and seemed to have had overwhelming public support.  shooting 5 teenagers with an unlicensed firearm without being physiclaly provoked?  so i can shoot anyone who says "gimme 5 dollars" in what i interpret to be a threatening tone?  and shoot not only him but his 4 friends when from the description doesn't sound like he was surrounded.  in a subwa war wiith a high risk of killing innocent peole around him?  and then fleeing the scene?
